  "worked_solution": "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061C$ and the given matrix $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u2061C=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(PD{P}^{-1})=P\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}\u2061D)\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u2061D=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}\u20612\\pi \\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061C & =P\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}\u2061D)\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
